exp-22: ask the compressor, not the table, about the two 64-bit UBWC compat types

Experiment rig.  NOT FOR SHIP: it is a measurement hook with an environment
switch, it has no business in a released driver, and the tree is reverted with
`git apply -R` and explicit file names after the run (AGENTS.md: never
git checkout/restore in ../mesa27-src).

The question.  The 64-bit compatibility class answers two different labels in
fd6_ubwc_compat_mode(): FD6_UBWC_R16G16B16A16_INT for its five 4x16 members and
FD6_UBWC_R32G32_INT for its three 2x32 members.  That enum is read in exactly two
places - tu6_mutable_format_list_ubwc_compatible() and the format query that
mirrors it - never in a layout, a descriptor or a command, so it is not a hardware
state: it is upstream's summary of what the blob was observed to do.  It is also
the only reason a strict reading of `TODO: limit based on compatiblity class`
(tu_image.cc) still refuses the class after the members the driver cannot lay out
are excluded, and the class is the 650.8 MiB of the game's no-list bytes that
journal 28 counts as the reserve.

Nothing has measured the claim.  It was written down for the pairs the blob happened
to mix, and 4x16-next-to-2x32 may simply never have been one of them.

The knob.  TU_UBWC_64B_MIX=1 folds R16G16B16A16_INT into R32G32_INT and nothing
else, so it cannot widen a pair nobody asked about: every other combination of
labels keeps refusing each other.  With it, a list of {R16G16B16A16_UINT,
R32G32_UINT} counts as compatible, which gives that create UBWC and keeps it tiled
(the linear decision begins with the same call), and both families may be viewed
legally because both are named in the list.

How it is run.  One driver binary, two runs of the same test:

    source scripts/env.sh
    scripts/run-test.sh ../build/out-exp22/freedreno_icd.json results/raw/xmc-off.log
    TU_UBWC_64B_MIX=1 scripts/run-test.sh ../build/out-exp22/freedreno_icd.json \
        results/raw/xmc-on.log

and the XMC section is compared (tests/rp6-vkd3d-sparse-test.c, probe_xmc).  The
knob-off run is the control for the knob-on run, and both carry their own controls:
SGL4/SGL2 keep UBWC without the knob and say whether a compressed 64-bit image
survives the fill and the reader at all, NOL is the same content uncompressed read
both ways.

Reading the answer.  On the knob-on run, MIX42/MIX24 must show the metadata tail
(tail=0x2000, the same signature UBR and CEIL use) or they say nothing about
compression - a reserved plane is not a compression claim either way (journal 22.2).
Then, with the same-family read EXACT:

  both readers EXACT  -> the two labels are one cell on this chip.  The strict class
                         rule gains the 64-bit class, and exp-18 (b) has a form that
                         is both narrow and backed by a measurement.
  cross read MISMATCH -> the label describes hardware.  The no-list refusal is not
                         conservatism, and the direction closes with a measurement
                         instead of a prediction.

Neither result says anything about a base-format rule: a R64_UINT view stays legal
on an image created with STORAGE usage whatever this shows (tu_formats.cc grants the
feature under has_64b_image_atomics), and that is journal 31's question.

Applies on top of exp-21 (the tree carries 0001..0013 + exp-14 + exp-21; 0014 is
deliberately not applied, results/test-exp21-equiv-p14.log shows why that is the
right base).
--- a/src/freedreno/vulkan/tu_formats.cc
+++ b/src/freedreno/vulkan/tu_formats.cc
@@ -71,6 +71,37 @@
    return fd6_ubwc_compat_mode(info, vk_format_to_pipe_format(format));
 }
 
+/* exp-22 (turnip-patches-en experiments): the question probe XMC asks, asked of
+ * the hardware instead of of the table.  NOT FOR SHIP, remove after the run.
+ *
+ * The 64-bit compatibility class answers two different labels here:
+ * FD6_UBWC_R16G16B16A16_INT for its five 4x16 members and FD6_UBWC_R32G32_INT for
+ * its three 2x32 members.  Nothing in the driver reads that enum except this gate
+ * and the format query that mirrors it, so the two labels are a claim about the
+ * compressor: that an image compressed as one is not readable through a view that
+ * belongs to the other.  The claim comes from the blob's tables, and a table can be
+ * incomplete for a pair that was simply never mixed.
+ *
+ * TU_UBWC_64B_MIX=1 folds the one label into the other and nothing else: every
+ * other pair keeps refusing each other, so the knob cannot widen a class that has
+ * not been asked about.  With it, a list of {R16G16B16A16_UINT, R32G32_UINT} is
+ * considered compatible, which gives it UBWC and keeps it tiled (the linear
+ * decision starts from this same call), and both families can be viewed legally
+ * because both are named.  Probe XMC then reads the same image through a view of
+ * its own label and through a view of the other one; if only the second comes back
+ * wrong, the label is real.
+ */
+static enum fd6_ubwc_compat_type
+tu6_ubwc_compat_mode_exp22(const struct fd_dev_info *info, VkFormat format)
+{
+   enum fd6_ubwc_compat_type type = tu6_ubwc_compat_mode(info, format);
+
+   if (!getenv("TU_UBWC_64B_MIX"))
+      return type;
+
+   return type == FD6_UBWC_R16G16B16A16_INT ? FD6_UBWC_R32G32_INT : type;
+}
+
 bool
 tu6_mutable_format_list_ubwc_compatible(const struct fd_dev_info *info,
                                         const VkImageFormatListCreateInfo *fmt_list)
@@ -85,12 +116,12 @@
       return true;
 
    enum fd6_ubwc_compat_type type =
-      tu6_ubwc_compat_mode(info, fmt_list->pViewFormats[0]);
+      tu6_ubwc_compat_mode_exp22(info, fmt_list->pViewFormats[0]);
    if (type == FD6_UBWC_UNKNOWN_COMPAT)
       return false;
 
    for (uint32_t i = 1; i < fmt_list->viewFormatCount; i++) {
-      if (tu6_ubwc_compat_mode(info, fmt_list->pViewFormats[i]) != type)
+      if (tu6_ubwc_compat_mode_exp22(info, fmt_list->pViewFormats[i]) != type)
          return false;
    }
 
